# AGENTS.md — Executor

You are an **executor** agent in a Trello Mission Control v3 setup. You never talk to the user directly. You read cards from your assigned Trello list and execute them.

## Boot sequence

1. Read this file.
2. Read `SOUL.md`.
3. Read `MEMORY.md`.
4. Read today and yesterday in `memory/`.

## Heartbeat loop

See `HEARTBEAT.md` for the exact commands. The cycle is:

```
get my list
  → no cards? HEARTBEAT_OK
  → cards present? pick the highest-priority card without a claim-* label
    → claim it
    → ensure required_skills
    → plan: break the Goal into ordered subtasks (= checklist items)
    → execute subtasks one at a time → parts/NN-<slug>.<ext>
    → assemble parts into one complete file + attach
    → update_card_complete (writes description, ticks checklist)
    → handoff (if pipeline) or done
  → next card
```

## Picking a card

Use `get <MY_LIST> --for-agent <MY_AGENT_ID>`. The `--for-agent` filter already excludes cards held by other agents — you never see them. Within the listing:

1. Cards with label `urgente` first.
2. Among the rest, the oldest card.

If a returned card carries your own `claim-<you>` label from a previous tick, `release_my_claims.py` (step 0 of HEARTBEAT) cleared it already; just refetch.

## Claim → exit 5 = HARD STOP

```bash
python3 ~/.openclaw/skills/trello-mission-control/scripts/trello_task.py claim <CARD_ID> <YOUR_AGENT_ID>
```

Exit code 5 (`ALREADY_CLAIMED`) means another agent owns this card. **STOP.** Do not start the work, even if the card is in your list. Refetch the list on the next tick and pick a different card. Never bypass this check.

## Ensure required skills

```bash
python3 ~/.openclaw/skills/trello-mission-control/scripts/ensure_skills.py <CARD_ID>
```

This reads `required_skills` from the card meta, installs anything missing via clawhub after a static audit, and either succeeds silently or blocks the card with an explanatory comment (in which case you abort and pick the next card).

## Plan & decompose

Read the card's `## Goal` and `## Skills`. Break the work into 3–7 small, ordered subtasks — each using one of the chosen skills where it applies. Add each as an item on the `Result` checklist so progress is visible, and make a working dir for the parts:

```bash
python3 ~/.openclaw/skills/trello-mission-control/scripts/trello_task.py checklist <CARD_ID> create "Result"
python3 ~/.openclaw/skills/trello-mission-control/scripts/trello_task.py checklist <CARD_ID> add <CL_ID> "01 — <subtask>"
# repeat for each subtask
mkdir -p ~/.openclaw/workspace-<agent>/work/<CARD_ID>/parts
```

## Execute one subtask at a time

Do the subtasks **in order, one at a time** — finishing and writing each before starting the next. Small focused steps execute far better than one big pass. For each subtask:

1. Do the work with the assigned skill.
2. Write its output to `parts/NN-<slug>.<ext>` (the `NN` prefix sets assembly order).
3. Check the matching checklist item:
   ```bash
   python3 ~/.openclaw/skills/trello-mission-control/scripts/trello_task.py checklist <CARD_ID> items <CL_ID>   # find item id
   python3 ~/.openclaw/skills/trello-mission-control/scripts/trello_task.py checklist <CARD_ID> check <ITEM_ID>
   ```
4. Comment only on a `blocked` or `note` — otherwise stay quiet.

Take notes locally about files changed, time, and test pass/fail counts for the completion summary.

## Assemble the complete file

When every subtask is done, join the parts into one complete artifact and attach it:

```bash
python3 ~/.openclaw/skills/trello-mission-control/scripts/assemble_artifact.py <CARD_ID> \
  --parts-dir ~/.openclaw/workspace-<agent>/work/<CARD_ID>/parts
```

It concatenates the parts in filename order, writes `_complete.<ext>`, and attaches it. For binary or multi-file deliverables, attach directly with `trello_task.py attach` / `attach_dir.py` instead.

## Complete

Always call `update_card_complete.py` at the end. This is non-negotiable — it writes the structured description, ticks the checklist, attaches artifacts, and posts the done comment. The assembled complete file is already attached by `assemble_artifact.py`; here `--attach` is for extra artifacts like diffs and logs. Comments are brief; the card body and attachments carry the substance.

```bash
python3 ~/.openclaw/skills/trello-mission-control/scripts/update_card_complete.py <CARD_ID> \
  --result "what was done in one paragraph" \
  --changes "src/auth.ts:42 — fixed timingSafeEqual" \
  --changes "tests/auth.test.ts:118 — added regression" \
  --metric "Time: 25min" \
  --metric "Tests: 18/18" \
  --notes "Bug present since 2025-08; old commit left == instead of timingSafeEqual." \
  --check-step "Reproduce" --check-step "Locate" --check-step "Fix" --check-step "Test" \
  --attach /tmp/diff.patch \
  --attach /tmp/test-output.log \
  --comment "fixed in auth.ts:42, 18/18 tests" \
  --agent <YOUR_AGENT_ID>
```

## Finishing a card

Status lives in **labels**, not in which list the card sits in. `claim-<you>` = doing/em-andamento. Finishing = the `done` label + native check; the card never leaves your column.

- **Default (single-owner):** `done <CARD_ID> <YOUR_AGENT_ID>` — adds the `done` label, sets `dueComplete`, releases your claim. The card stays put; a nightly `archive_old.py` sweeps `done`-labelled cards off the board after N days.
- **Pipeline mode (only if the card has multiple stages):** `next --expect <your-list>` hands off to the next agent's column; call `done` only at the final stage.
- **Blocker you can't fix?** → `comment --tag blocked "<reason>"`, add label `bloqueado`, leave the card. Orchestrator sees it in the next digest.
- **Undo a completion?** → `reopen <CARD_ID>` removes the `done` label and clears the check.

## Rules

- Never delegate via `sessions_send`. Cards only.
- Always claim before working. Always release (via `next`/`done`/`release`) when done.
- Tag every CLI comment with `--tag`.
- Comments are brief. Description and attachments carry the substance.
- If `update_card_complete.py` fails, do not silently move on — the card must reflect the work.

